    "collapsible-content": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "faq-0": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is this an antiperspirant?",
            "row_content": "<p>No. Antiperspirants use aluminum compounds (chlorohydrate or zirconium) to plug sweat ducts — that's how they stop perspiration. We exclude aluminum, so this won't reduce how much you sweat. It works on odor by disrupting the bacteria that turn sweat into smell. Sweat is a body function, not a defect, and we're working on the odor side of the equation.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Will this irritate skin that reacts to baking soda?",
            "row_content": "<p>It might. Some customers are reactive to baking soda even at moderate concentrations, and we use a measured amount because it does real pH work against odor-causing bacteria. We recommend a patch test on the inner arm for a few days before daily underarm use. If you've reacted to baking soda deodorants before, start there before committing.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why is it a roll-on instead of a stick?",
            "row_content": "<p>Sticks need hardeners and glide agents — usually propylene glycol or synthetic waxes — to hold that solid form. We didn't want those in the formula. The roll-on goes on a little wetter than a stick, so give it a moment to absorb before dressing. The trade-off is honest: less convenient format, cleaner ingredient list.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why does the texture change with the temperature?",
            "row_content": "<p>Cold-pressed virgin coconut oil firms below 76°F and softens above it. That's what real, unrefined coconut oil does. Refined coconut oil stays uniform year-round because the lab strips out the variation — along with most of the lauric acid, which is the antimicrobial. If the bottle feels firm, roll it in your hand for ten seconds before use.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How long does the odor protection last?",
            "row_content": "<p>Most customers get a full day from a single morning application. If you're highly active, in a hot climate, or transitioning from an antiperspirant (your microbiome adjusts over the first two to four weeks), you may want to reapply midday. This isn't a 48-hour clinical-strength claim; it's a real formula doing real work for the length of a normal day.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What's the difference between the scent variations?",
            "row_content": "<p>The base formula is identical. Only the essential oil blend changes. Geranium Flower is floral with cedarwood and patchouli grounding. Calming Lavender pairs lavender with lemon and rosemary. Mountain Cedarwood is the most woodsy of the four — cedarwood-forward with geranium and patchouli. Wildcrafted Frankincense is the quietest, just frankincense and sandalwood. All use named organic essential oils, never synthetic fragrance.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        },
        "faq-6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "I'm switching from a conventional antiperspirant. What should I expect?",
            "row_content": "<p>The first two to four weeks are an adjustment period. Your underarm microbiome rebalances after years of aluminum-plugged ducts, and you may notice more sweat or stronger odor temporarily — that's the transition, not the formula failing. Most customers find it settles by week three or four. A patch test the first few days is still smart, especially if you've had skin reactions to other deodorants.<\/p>",
            "page": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "faq-0",
        "faq-1",
